<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Peter II of Yugoslavia</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1923">1923</a>–<a href="/years/1970">1970</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Peter II was the last king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yugoslavia">Yugoslavia</a>, succeeding his father <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-i-of-russia">Alexander I</a> in 1934 under a regency. In March 1941 officers who overthrew the regency's government declared him of age. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> invaded weeks later and he went into exile in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>. The communists abolished the monarchy in 1945.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/peter-ii-of-yugoslavia.jpg" alt="Peter II of Yugoslavia" width="320" height="557" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Непознат · CC BY 3.0 rs · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kralj_Petar_II.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Peter was born on 6 September 1923 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgrade">Belgrade</a>, the eldest son of Alexander I of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yugoslavia">Yugoslavia</a> and Maria of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romania">Romania</a>. His godparents included King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-v">George V of the United Kingdom</a>, for whom the Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/york">York</a> stood proxy. He was tutored at the Royal Palace and then attended Sandroyd School in Surrey. On 9 October 1934, at the age of eleven, he succeeded to the throne after his father was assassinated in Marseille during a state visit to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, and a regency was set up under his father's cousin Prince Paul.</p><p>Paul held that he must hand the kingdom to Peter unaltered when he turned eighteen, and he resisted attempts to revise the 1931 constitution. On 20 August 1939 he did allow an agreement between the prime minister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dragisa-cvetkovic">Dragiša Cvetković</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vladko-macek">Vladko Maček</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croatian-peasant-party">Croatian Peasant Party</a>, which created a Banovina of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croatia">Croatia</a> with substantial autonomy. It was very unpopular with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbs">Serbs</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Prince Paul declared on 25 March 1941 that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yugoslavia">Yugoslavia</a> would join the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tripartite-pact">Tripartite Pact</a>. Two days later General <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dusan-simovic">Dušan Simović</a> led a pro-British coup in the name of the seventeen-year-old Peter, who climbed down a drain-pipe to greet the rebels. Simović saluted him as king, and the crowds in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgrade">Belgrade</a> cheered him. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> attacked on 6 April 1941; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luftwaffe">Luftwaffe</a> bombed Belgrade, killing between 3,000 and 4,000 people, and the government surrendered on 17 April. Italy, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> and Germany annexed parts of the country, and the rest was governed by two German-controlled puppet governments.</p><p>Peter reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> in June 1941, where a government-in-exile was set up. His engagement to Princess Alexandra of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a>, announced in July 1943, caused controversy at home, since Serbian tradition held that a leader must not marry during a national emergency. He dismissed the prime minister Slobodan Jovanović, who opposed the announcement. The couple married in March 1944.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In November 1944 the prime minister Šubašić agreed with Stalin that Peter would not return until a plebiscite was held, and that a three-man regency council would govern meanwhile; Peter objected that he was twenty-one and needed no regent. The Constituent Assembly, elected on 11 November 1945 amid reported fraud and intimidation, deposed him on 29 November 1945 and proclaimed a republic. Peter settled in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> and lived much of his exile in the Chicago area.</p><p>He suffered from depression and alcoholism, and developed cirrhosis of the liver. He died in Denver on 3 November 1970 after a failed liver transplant and was buried at the Saint Sava Monastery Church in Libertyville, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/illinois">Illinois</a>. His remains were returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgrade">Belgrade</a> on 22 January 2013 and buried at Oplenac on 26 May 2013.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Peter II of Yugoslavia” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgrade">Belgrade</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/serbia">Serbia</a>. Died in Denver, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-states">the United States</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=44.82,20.46&amp;year=1953" aria-label="Belgrade on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Peter II of Yugoslavia"><image href="/maps/globes/g030n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4424 3723h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4424 3723h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=44.82,20.46&amp;year=1953">What was here in 1953? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
